messages.content:
{
 "observed": [
  "Wednesday, February 3, 2016. The newsreader gives the date twice. The depot clerk tears the Tuesday sheet off his timetable.",
  "You are at the radio station. A copy of a received telegram is at your hand: Alma's daughter in Pittsburgh has the whole interview. Her reply is 'I know that voice. Play it again.'"
 ],
 "your_retained_experiences": [
  "Rita records Alma at the Rialto and puts her recording on the patch.",
  "Rita takes the regional line. Alma's complete interview reaches her daughter in Pittsburgh.",
  "Get her voice to her daughter before the room goes dark. One tape, one number, one evening. Alma promised it. I made it. The line is open now and it will not be open tomorrow. I don't need to dance. I don't need to take notes for the party. I need this tape to land in Pittsburgh tonight, in a woman's hands who has been waiting, and I need to be the one who pressed the key, not a machine, not a relay, not a 'we'll get to it Thursday.' I pressed it. It's done. I can stand up now."
 ],
 "physical_choices": {
  "keep_own_copy": "Label a personal copy of the transmitted reel.",
  "go_off_duty": "Put the kit away and go off duty.",
  "listen_to_interview": "Listen to the transmitted reel on the radio."
 }
}

{
 "action": "listen_to_interview",
 "detail": "I thread the reel onto the studio monitor head, the one with the sticky tape still on the flange where I spliced it at 11 p.m. I set the volume to the middle notch, not the loud one, not the whisper one. The needle drops. A breath. Then Alma's voice, the one I caught in the back booth of the Rialto with the projector warm behind her and the carbon smell in her coat. She says the thing about the 35-millimetre gate, the way she says it, the little click of her tongue before the word. I let it run to the end. The tape hits the end-stop and the arm swings up. I pull the reel off. I do not rewind it. I set the reel on the desk, on top of the telegram, the one that says *I know that voice. Play it again.* I press the machine's kill switch. The little red light goes out. I stand up. I put the spare spools and the scissors and the tape in the kit bag, zip it, hang it on the hook by the door. I take my coat off the peg. I don't say anything to the room. I walk out. The depot clerk is already tearing tomorrow's sheet off his board. I don't look at him. I take the stairs down, not the lift. The street is wet and the sodium light makes everything look like a still frame. I walk east. I don't go to the festival. I don't go to the bar where they're going to toast the season. I walk until my hands stop being cold. Then I go home. The tape is in the kit bag. It will sit there. It is done. It is in Pittsburgh. A woman heard her mother and said *again* and will say it as many times as she wants, in her own kitchen, in her own dark. That is not my job anymore. I pressed the key. I am the one who pressed it. I can go home now.",
 "intention": "Hear Alma's voice once, fully, under my own power, in a room that is mine, before the night closes and the machine cools. Then close the machine. Then leave. The obligation is finished. I do not owe the evening a second performance.",
 "words": "(none to anyone. Only the hiss of the tape, then the silence after the end-stop, then the click of the kill switch, then the zip of the kit bag, then the street.)"
}
